Beryllium: own profile directory, branding and FreeBSD system paths

Purpose: without this patch a Beryllium build would use ~/.config/chromium
  and share a profile with the stock FreeBSD Chromium package, read
  Chromium's system policy, extension and native-messaging directories, and
  carry Helium's company and product names in BRANDING (owner decision Q1:
  Beryllium does not present itself as Helium).
  - Profile: ~/.config/beryllium; override with $BERYLLIUM_CONFIG_HOME
    (was ~/.config/chromium and $CHROME_CONFIG_HOME).
  - Desktop file name: beryllium.desktop; override with $BERYLLIUM_DESKTOP.
  - BSD system paths (from the imported FreeBSD patches):
    /usr/local/share/chromium/extensions -> /usr/local/share/beryllium/extensions,
    /usr/local/etc/chromium/native-messaging-hosts -> /usr/local/etc/beryllium/native-messaging-hosts,
    /usr/local/etc/chromium/policies -> /usr/local/etc/beryllium/policies,
    ~/.cache/chromium (created by the FreeBSD sandbox stub) -> ~/.cache/beryllium.
  - BRANDING: Beryllium names; the macOS-only identifiers are neutral.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on: the imported BSD patches for
  chrome_paths.cc, policy_paths.cc, channel_info_posix.cc and
  sandbox_freebsd.cc (stage 2), and Helium's change-chromium-branding.patch
  and scan-chrome-native-messaging-hosts.patch (stage 3).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8 (www/chromium 156.0.8078.47_1), using
  freebsd/tools/patch-workbench.
Test: freebsd/tests/functional (planned): a fresh start creates
  ~/.config/beryllium and ~/.cache/beryllium and nothing under
  ~/.config/chromium or ~/.cache/chromium; a policy file in
  /usr/local/etc/beryllium/policies/managed is honoured and one in
  /usr/local/etc/chromium/policies is not; chrome://version (shown as
  beryllium://version) names Beryllium.
security-review: required (changes where policy is read from)

